The PACS is well suited to two distinct clinical uses. The first is progress monitoring: administering the PACS repeatedly throughout treatment allows clinicians to track whether craving levels are responding to intervention. A reduction of 3 or more points across administrations indicates a clinically meaningful improvement, while an increase of the same magnitude may signal heightened relapse risk warranting closer attention. The second use is severity assessment: a single administration can help establish where a client sits relative to others in treatment and whether craving is likely to be an active clinical concern. Because the PACS asks about urges and thoughts about drinking rather than actual consumption, it can also yield useful information in situations where a client has not yet disclosed, or is reluctant to discuss, their drinking behaviour. For a broader picture of alcohol-related harm and dependence, the PACS pairs well with the Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test (
AUDIT
) and the Leeds Dependence Questionnaire (
LDQ
).

